feat(mcp): add observe_tool_call (#206)

The largest block of shell in the reference hook was not the MCP calls. It was
the derivation around them: `shasum -a 256` over the body, then
`$LOG/<run_id>/<digest>.json` written by hand. Every other harness would have
to reimplement both byte-exactly, because internal/api/runlog.go and
internal/reconciler/writes.go re-derive that path from a digest in the chain
and check the file against it — a layout off by one character produces a run
whose every body reads as missing.

So the layout is PORTED, not designed: one directory per run, one file per
digest, the 64 hex characters as the name, the body's own bytes as the content.
The two readers are the specification; this is the second writer of it.

Bodies are written and never sent. doc 05 keeps tool-call bodies on the
operator's machine, so the only two things that leave this tool are the digest,
which goes into the chain, and a boolean. MCP-046 checks that against the
ledger, the idempotency store, the reply and the error text — and separately
against observe.go's own import list, because a test that watches one call can
be satisfied by a leak on a path that call did not take.

No `tool_call` for a body that was not kept. I3 admits no action without a
record; the converse is the order in store(): the body is written first, and
the event appended only after the write returned. An event naming a digest
whose body never landed would be a permanent claim about evidence that never
existed, and the reconciler would later count it as an uncorroborated write.
The reverse leftover — a body whose append then failed — is an unreferenced
file the retention window collects.

Run-token enforcement is get_credential's, from the one implementation of it:
an unknown run and a bad token are answered with the same class and the same
message, before the run directory is consulted, because a run_id is public.
No new error class — the vocabulary is protected (doc 08 §3), so an unwritable
volume reports LEDGER_UNAVAILABLE, retryable, which is the one class of the
eleven that gives the caller the instruction the situation warrants.

The tool takes no idempotency_key (IP §4 gives it none) while ADR-0004 requires
one on the tool_call it appends; the key is derived from (run_id, digest), the
pair doc 01 §4 makes this tool idempotent on, so a caller cannot record one
observation twice under two keys.

Driven by MCP-044..MCP-049, written first and observed failing on the undefined
names before any production code existed. 100% of statements on the package,
observe.go included, with every error return driven — the unwritable volume by
a real filesystem refusal rather than a stub.

